   "l": "Know the male,\nhold to the female:\nbe the ravine of all-under-heaven.\nBe the ravine of all-under-heaven,\nand the abiding potency does not leave;\nyou return again to the newborn.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Know what in you is male, and hold to what is female; be the ravine of all under heaven. Be that ravine, and the potency that abides never leaves you; you go back again to the newborn child.",
   "n": "雄 and 雌 are bird words — cock and hen — before they are anything else, and the pair is about position rather than sex: Wang Bi glosses 雄 as what goes in front and 雌 as what comes behind. To know the one and keep to the other is to understand the leading part well enough to decline it. 谿 is the gully a hill stream cuts, and the figure is the book's constant one — the low place is where everything arrives. Notice the pivot: 為天下谿 closes one line and opens the next, a hinge this chapter swings on three times and chapter 16's ladder used at every rung. 常德, 'abiding potency', is built exactly like chapter 1's 常道 and is no more a fixed term than that was. One more thing the English cannot show: 其 in 知其雄 has no antecedent anywhere — the Chinese simply does not say whose male and whose female these are. We read it as the reader's own, which is how Wang Bi's 'the sage puts himself behind' takes it; read instead as the world's, the line becomes advice on how to handle other men, and nothing in the grammar rules that out. Sound: the stanza's line-ends 雌 cí, 谿 xī and 兒 ér fell together in one Old Chinese rhyme group, and Mandarin has pulled them apart.",
   "p": true
  },
