   "l": "Know the white,\nhold to the black:\nbe the pattern of all-under-heaven.",
   "v": true,
   "n": "Here is the chapter's textual problem, and it is a real one. The received text runs three stanzas: male/female → ravine, white/black → pattern, glory/disgrace → valley. The Mawangdui manuscripts order the three differently and read 知其白，守其辱 — white against disgrace — where our text pairs 榮 'glory' with 辱. Qing philologists argued in detail that this white/black stanza is a later insertion: its vocabulary (式, 忒, 無極) is unlike the rest of the chapter, and 白 'the lit' pairs naturally with 辱 'the dirtied' in the older arrangement. The case is strong, not decisive; our edition prints all three stanzas, so we print all three and tell you the argument. 白 and 黑 are not good and evil here — they are the conspicuous and the obscure, and the instruction is the one already given: know the showy part, take up the dim one. (That manuscript detail is reported from the secondary literature rather than checked here against a printed transcription of the silk texts; treat it as the standard account rather than as something we have verified.)"
