   ],
   "l": "Know the glory,\nhold to the disgrace:\nbe the valley of all-under-heaven.\nThe abiding potency is then enough,\nand you return again to the uncarved block.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Know what glory is but keep to disgrace, and be the valley of all under heaven. Then the potency that abides is full, and you go back again to the uncarved block.",
   "n": "乃 is chapter 16's linking word, 'and then it follows'. 樸 is this book's own figure and the one place where we keep the standard English rendering, because it is exact: timber as it left the tree, still able to become anything, which is precisely what a finished thing can no longer do. Sound: 谷 gǔ, 足 zú and 樸 pǔ belonged to a single Old Chinese rhyme group and still chime in Mandarin — the same run you heard closing chapter 19. The stanza before this one rhymed 式, 忒 and 極, which Mandarin has scattered into shì, tè and jí."
  },
