   "l": "The uncarved block broken up becomes vessels;\nthe sage puts that to use,\nand so makes officers and chiefs.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Break the uncarved block up and you have vessels; the sage puts the breaking to use, and out of it makes officers and chiefs.",
   "n": "為 twice in three lines, both times wéi: first 'becomes', then 'makes'. The second line is read two ways. Taken flatly, 則為官長 says the sage 'becomes chief of the officers'. Wang Bi takes it the other way — once the block is scattered, the hundred kinds of conduct come out and the several sorts of men appear, like so many vessels, and the sage, following that scattering, sets up officers over them. We follow him, as the house rule requires when Wang Bi and a smoother reading part company. Either way the admission is the same: the sage does not refuse the carved world; he governs it."
