   "l": "Therefore the great cutting-out does no cutting.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "So the greatest ordering severs nothing.",
   "n": "The last line is a pun, and the chapter's point. 制 is a cutting word — to cut cloth out to a pattern — which is how it came to be the ordinary word for regulating and governing, and governing is its sense here; the English 'cutting-out' in the literal line is there to keep the pun visible, not because the line is about cloth. 割 is cutting in the plain sense, hacking a thing into parts. The great ordering is the one that shapes without severing: it can make officers out of the scattered block precisely because it never cuts anything away from itself. Wang Bi: the great ordering takes the heart of all under heaven for its own heart, and so there is no cutting in it."
